<p>ActiveCampaign has earned its reputation as one of the best email automation platforms on the market. Its visual workflow builder is powerful, its deliverability is solid, and it has built a loyal following among marketers who live and breathe email sequences. If email automation is the centre of your universe, ActiveCampaign deserves a serious look.</p>

<p>But most small business owners do not live and breathe email sequences. They need email, yes. They also need a CRM, a booking system, a way to collect reviews, SMS follow-ups, and landing pages that convert. And that is where the conversation gets more interesting.</p>

<p>Comparing DUSA to ActiveCampaign is useful because the two platforms represent two different philosophies. One is a specialist. The other is a platform. Both have their place.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>What ActiveCampaign does well</h2>

<p>ActiveCampaign's visual automation builder is best in class. That is not an exaggeration. The ability to create complex, branching workflows with conditional logic, wait steps, split testing, and goal tracking is impressive. If you need a 15-step onboarding sequence that adapts based on user behaviour, ActiveCampaign handles it beautifully.</p>

<p>The email deliverability is also strong. ActiveCampaign invests heavily in sender reputation management, and the platform consistently ranks near the top in independent deliverability tests. For businesses that send high volumes of email, that matters.</p>

<p>The CRM, while not its primary selling point, is decent. It includes deal pipelines, lead scoring, and contact management. For a tool that started as an email platform, the CRM has come a long way. If you are building your <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email marketing strategy</a> and want deep automation, ActiveCampaign delivers on that specific promise.</p>

<h2>Where ActiveCampaign leaves gaps for small business</h2>

<p>The gaps appear when you zoom out from email and look at everything else a small business needs to operate. ActiveCampaign does not include a funnel or landing page builder. It does not have a built-in appointment booking system. It does not offer review management. SMS is available, but only as an add-on with usage-based pricing.</p>

<p>That means a typical small business using ActiveCampaign still needs to bolt on three or four other tools. A landing page builder like Leadpages or Unbounce. A booking tool like Calendly. A review platform like Podium. An SMS service. Each of those comes with its own subscription, its own login, and its own learning curve. For a deeper look at what happens when you stack tools like this, see our breakdown of <a href="/blog/5-tools-to-replace">five tools you can replace with one platform</a>.</p>

<p>The pricing also escalates. ActiveCampaign starts at $29 USD per month on the Starter plan, which sounds reasonable. But the Starter plan is limited. You do not get landing pages, lead scoring, or advanced automation features. The Plus plan at $49 per month adds some of those, but the real power sits behind the Pro plan at $149 per month. And even at that price, you are still missing the funnel builder, booking system, and review management that you need to run separately.</p>

<blockquote><p>The cost of a tool is not just its subscription price. It is the total of every other tool you need alongside it to run your business.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Where DUSA takes a different approach</h2>

<p>DUSA is not trying to be the most advanced email automation tool on the market. It is trying to be the most complete growth platform for small business. Those are different goals, and they lead to different design decisions.</p>

<p>DUSA includes email marketing with automation workflows. You can build multi-step sequences, set triggers based on contact behaviour, and <a href="/blog/automate-follow-up">automate your follow-up</a> without thinking about it. For the vast majority of small businesses, the automation capabilities are more than sufficient.</p>

<p>But DUSA also includes everything else. Funnel and landing page builder. Appointment booking. SMS. Review management. CRM with pipeline tracking. All in one login, one subscription, one platform. There is no conversation about which add-ons you need or which third-party tools to connect.</p>

<p>The trade-off is real, though. If you need automation workflows with 20 conditional branches, split paths, and predictive sending optimisation, ActiveCampaign gives you more depth in that specific area. DUSA covers the automation needs of most small businesses, but it does not try to match ActiveCampaign's most advanced email features.</p>

